{
  "requirement": "The bundled skills MUST direct agents to describe sites exactly as the site queries select them, to state as an obligation any convention the queries rely on, such as how a list is named, and to state only per-site obligations that a reviewer can check from the site and the helpers it calls.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has three guidance obligations for the bundled skills. All three are checked by the one marked test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_describe_sites_as_queries_select_them_with_checkable_obligations]`.\n\n1. **Describe sites exactly as the site queries select them.** The test reads the `## Do` section of the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md` through `distributed_reference` and `section`. It asserts that the section contains \"Describe sites exactly as the site queries select them.\" It also reads the `## Don't` section and asserts \"Don't describe sites more broadly than the queries select\". Together these cover the positive direction and the \"no broader\" direction of \"exactly\".\n2. **State any convention the queries rely on as an obligation.** The `## Do` assertion also requires \"When a query relies on a convention, such as how a variable is named, state the convention as an obligation\". The requirement's own example is a list name, and the test's example is a variable name. Both are examples of a convention, so this makes no difference to the obligation.\n3. **State only per-site obligations a reviewer can check from the site and the helpers it calls.** The `## Don't` assertion requires \"Don't state a per-site obligation that a reviewer cannot check from the site and the helpers it calls\".\n\nThe helpers enforce these checks. `assert_text` panics, with a label and the missing phrase, when a phrase is absent from the text. `section` fails if the heading is missing, and it stops at the next heading of the same or a higher level outside a code fence. This confines each phrase to its intended section.\n\nThe check is phrase presence, not semantics. That is the project's normal way to test guidance text, and these phrases carry the requirement's wording. The requirement says \"the bundled skills\", but the guidance has one home: the habeas skill's distributed-requirements reference. Other tests in the file point to that reference from `SKILL.md`. I found no other skill that needs separate coverage. There are no acknowledgments, and no gap was found.",
